WEBVTT

00:00:00.568 --> 00:00:26.800
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today a B.C. plywood mill tells Global News it can't absorb a new fifty-percent U.S. tariff — the exact plants that did everything right, moving up the value chain, getting hit hardest. Plus, hundreds of thousands of barred owls slated for removal to save the spotted owl. Who's allowed to act when things go wrong, and who just waits? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:42.775 --> 00:01:06.343
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Tuesday, August 25th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry.

00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:19.087
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're talking forestry, ranching, mining, and the machinery of the resource economy today — who profits when it adapts, and who gets left holding the bag when it doesn't.

00:01:19.888 --> 00:01:46.056
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into it. Horsefly Lake is sitting at 14 degrees with clear skies this morning, high of 29 expected, low of 12 tonight, with a light drizzle moving in tomorrow. Around the region — 100 Mile House at 13 and clear, Williams Lake at 14 and clear, Quesnel at 14 and clear, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Anahim Lake is sitting at 11 with mostly clear skies.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.956
<v Riley>Good smoke-clearing weather, for once. Let's get into the news.

00:01:52.356 --> 00:02:23.852
<v Casey>Four stories close to home this morning, a long theme block behind them, so we've got ground to cover. 100 Mile House District General Hospital confirmed its emergency department will be closed until eight tonight, August 24th. That's a full day without emergency intake at the only hospital serving the South Cariboo. All other inpatient services continue as normal, but anyone with a life-threatening emergency during that window is being told to call 911 for transport to the nearest available facility.

00:02:24.652 --> 00:02:30.060
<v Riley>Nearest available facility likely means Williams Lake — close to an hour for much of that catchment.

00:02:31.329 --> 00:02:45.377
<v Casey>Staying with emergency response, a family in Lac La Hache put out a lightning-sparked fire on Emerald Island themselves, before crews could get out there. The 100 Mile Free Press reports it was contained to a single tree and never spread.

00:02:46.658 --> 00:03:14.658
<v Riley>From one small fire to a much bigger one. The Williams Lake Tribune reports fire activity could increase this week at the Pear Lake blaze in the Cariboo, which has now grown to a hundred and sixty-four thousand, seven hundred and forty-five hectares. BC Wildfire Service crews are working containment lines on the south flank off Highway 97, but the Tribune's reporting suggests weather this week could push activity back up rather than down.

00:03:15.858 --> 00:03:21.314
<v Casey>A hundred and sixty-four thousand hectares. That's not a typo, and it isn't slowing down.

00:03:22.737 --> 00:03:38.817
<v Riley>Further south, evacuation orders and alerts have been downgraded for the Ainslie Creek wildfire west of the Coquihalla. And the Fraser Valley Regional District rescinded an alert tied to the Brunswick Complex fire on Sunday, after activity there eased off.

00:03:40.214 --> 00:03:47.102
<v Casey>Downgraded is welcome. It's still the last week of August with a lot of open fire on the map.

00:03:49.101 --> 00:04:14.597
<v Riley>On the industry side, the Western Producer has a number worth sitting with. Canada now has fifty-two thousand fewer people working in agricultural jobs than it did six years ago — the survey breaks agriculture into sub-categories, crop production among them, and they're all shrinking. Farmers have been saying this anecdotally for years: finding help for calving, for harvest, for anything time-sensitive keeps getting harder.

00:04:15.097 --> 00:04:20.313
<v Casey>Which raises the obvious question of who runs these operations in another decade.

00:04:21.776 --> 00:04:37.016
<v Riley>Robotics and remote sensing get pitched as the answer, but that equipment assumes skilled labour on-site to run and maintain it. Somebody still has to troubleshoot a soil moisture sensor at two in the morning during calving — and that's the worker this decline is losing.

00:04:38.487 --> 00:04:59.735
<v Casey>Tom's Hardware reports the Cherokee Nation has banned hyperscale data center development outright on its tribally owned and trust lands. The Nation has more than four hundred seventy-five thousand citizens and says it won't support any such project without direct consultation, citing energy and water consumption, air quality, noise, and cultural resource protection.

00:05:00.635 --> 00:05:13.635
<v Riley>That's a Nation with the legal standing and staff capacity to say no and make it stick. Not every Nation negotiating a data centre or a mine has that leverage yet, which is why other governments will study this one closely.

00:05:15.702 --> 00:05:32.470
<v Casey>Meanwhile in the U.S., Tyson Foods says it will close or sell three beef plant and packaging sites. The Western Producer notes it's part of a broader shrinking of Tyson's beef footprint as a historic U.S. cattle shortage keeps eating into margins for the country's largest meatpacker.

00:05:33.951 --> 00:05:39.895
<v Riley>If Tyson can't make the math work with all that scale, margins are thin from feedlot to packer.

00:05:41.138 --> 00:05:57.706
<v Casey>A Summerland farmer in the Okanagan was twelve hours away from harvesting a crop when a wildfire broke out nearby. Global News notes the region already absorbed a heat dome in 2021 and a cold snap in 2024 that shut down the B.C. Tree Fruits Cooperative.

00:05:59.089 --> 00:06:07.769
<v Riley>Three climate shocks to the same growing region inside five years. Crop insurers are already pricing that into premiums for anyone farming that valley.

00:06:09.028 --> 00:06:28.500
<v Casey>The Walrus has a longer read on the vanishing golden age of the con artist. Even Frank Abagnale's own life story turned out to be mostly invented — a scammer's biography as the biggest scam of all. Phishing links and industrial-scale fraud have replaced the silver-tongued grifter.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:44.450
<v Riley>ArchDaily has a piece on "Loose Fit," an exhibition of provisional, participatory architecture from East Asia responding to climate emergency and resource scarcity — buildings designed to be adapted or dismantled rather than built to last forever.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:52.004
<v Casey>(wry) Out here the problem runs the other way. Buildings meant to be temporary somehow become permanent.

00:06:53.179 --> 00:07:13.211
<v Riley>The Western Producer also flagged agriculture's fears as the U.S. tariff clock keeps ticking, and the honey sector's already feeling it. The Canadian Beekeepers Federation says the country's largest honey packer cancelled producer contracts because its American sales are gone. Eighty percent of Canada's honey comes from Prairie producers who depend on that market channel.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:19.258
<v Casey>And honey isn't the only piece of the resource economy caught in that squeeze — more on that shortly.

00:07:20.617 --> 00:07:31.081
<v Riley>The U.S. Army awarded a contract worth close to fifty million dollars for machines that pull drinking water out of humid air, meant for troops operating far from reliable supply lines.

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:38.213
<v Casey>(deadpan) Fifty million for water from air. Most of the Cariboo would settle for highway cell coverage.

00:07:39.365 --> 00:07:54.253
<v Riley>Hyperallergic profiled an artist named Dean Millien, whose aluminum-foil animal sculptures are a highlight of the "Greater New York" show at MoMA PS1 — a practice he started as a kid in Brooklyn, working with the cheapest material in the house.

00:07:55.405 --> 00:08:09.293
<v Casey>ScienceDaily reports researchers have found that triple-negative breast cancer tumours may recruit the body's own immune cells to build a nerve network that helps the tumour grow, a discovery that could eventually open new treatment targets.

00:08:10.605 --> 00:08:21.845
<v Riley>And Fast Company has the number on the California trial against Meta over child safety on its platforms — states are seeking damages that could climb as high as one point four trillion dollars.

00:08:23.074 --> 00:08:31.562
<v Casey>A number that big is theatre until a judge rules. Speaking of things run by the people closest to them, let's get into today's spotlight.

00:08:32.961 --> 00:09:09.753
<v Casey>Today we're spotlighting the Mt. Timothy Ski Society, the community-founded group behind Mt. Timothy Recreation Resort near Lac La Hache. It's a volunteer-driven hill with thirty-five runs, a tube park, and family skiing that serves Williams Lake, Quesnel, and 100 Mile House alike. It exists because local people decided decades ago the South Cariboo needed a ski hill it could run itself, without a conglomerate calling the shots from somewhere else. Lift tickets stay affordable, the board is local, and the money that comes in stays in the operation. More at skitimothy.com.

00:09:10.438 --> 00:09:18.126
<v Riley>(warmly) One of the few places around here where "community-owned" isn't marketing language — it's the whole operating model, right down to who sits on the board.

00:09:18.726 --> 00:09:26.966
<v Casey>Which is a decent bridge into what we're digging into today: who owns the operation, and who gets to make the call when something goes wrong with it.

00:09:28.366 --> 00:10:02.366
<v Riley>A B.C. plywood manufacturer told Global News last week it cannot absorb the new fifty-percent U.S. tariff on plywood, paper, fibreboard, particleboard, and veneered wood panels. That tariff took effect around August 19th, stacked on top of existing softwood lumber duties. CIBC analysts point out raw softwood lumber is exempt from this particular round — it's the finished panel products taking the hit. At Richmond Plywood, the tariff threatens roughly a fifth of total sales, concentrated in the high-end products where the margin actually lives.

00:10:02.939 --> 00:10:13.747
<v Casey>So the mill can retool, get more efficient, invest in better kiln technology, do everything the innovation crowd tells it to do — and none of it matters if Washington shuts the door.

00:10:14.354 --> 00:10:31.810
<v Riley>That's the part the modernization talk skips. This round of tariffs is expected to touch roughly twenty-eight billion dollars in Canadian exports. A mill can improve its grading systems and its process efficiency and still lose the whole play because of a decision made in a country where nobody on that floor has a vote.

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:46.010
<v Casey>So who's allowed to fix a problem like this, and who just has to absorb it? A mill worker in Quesnel gets no say on tariff policy. Neither does the owner, most of the time, however well-run the operation is.

00:10:46.603 --> 00:11:05.131
<v Riley>And the leverage that does exist isn't distributed evenly. B.C.'s premier has declined to make concessions — putting U.S. alcohol back on shelves, for one — without reciprocal tariff relief for forestry. That's leverage exercised at the provincial level on behalf of an industry holding almost none of its own.

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:13.438
<v Casey>Leverage the mill floor will never hold. Decisions made elsewhere, felt here, on a lag nobody controls.

00:11:14.078 --> 00:11:27.518
<v Riley>The detail that surprised me: the tariff exempts raw softwood lumber but hits the value-added panel products hardest. That's backwards from any policy goal about pushing processing closer to the resource instead of shipping raw logs south.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:40.918
<v Casey>So the operations that did what they were told for twenty years — stop shipping raw logs, build the plant, capture the margin locally — get punished harder than the ones still exporting unprocessed timber.

00:11:41.535 --> 00:11:52.687
<v Riley>Richmond Plywood made the value-chain climb every economic development plan in this province has been pushing for two decades. And the finished product turns out to be the exposed one.

00:11:53.187 --> 00:12:01.043
<v Casey>Which is an argument against betting a local economy on any single export market, no matter how sophisticated the mill gets.

00:12:01.715 --> 00:12:17.915
<v Riley>Diversification is the obvious answer, but on what timeline? Domestic Canadian panel demand isn't absorbing this plant's volume overnight. Certification and relationships in other export markets — Japan, parts of Europe — take years, not months.

00:12:18.581 --> 00:12:25.381
<v Casey>And layoffs happen on the mill's runway, not the market's. Two clocks, completely different speeds.

00:12:26.081 --> 00:12:39.545
<v Riley>A mill losing a fifth of its sales has months of runway before that forces a staffing decision. A new customer relationship in Japan takes years. Those numbers don't reconcile, and nobody's pretending they do.

00:12:40.119 --> 00:13:03.119
<v Casey>There's a parallel in a fight south of the border. The U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service is running a program calling for lethal removal of hundreds of thousands of barred owls — an invasive competitor to the northern spotted owl — across the Pacific Northwest. Senate attempts to block it failed. Only specially trained, permitted individuals may carry out the removals.

00:13:03.719 --> 00:13:18.335
<v Riley>The spotted owl was the totem of the whole Timber Wars in the late eighties and early nineties. Sacred to forest defenders, a curse word in logging towns. Now it's back as a species-versus-species cull rather than a fight about logging at all.

00:13:18.947 --> 00:13:46.571
<v Casey>Friends of Animals has sued to halt the removals, arguing it sets a precedent — killing one species to protect another, indefinitely, as federal policy. Conservation groups counter that barred owls will wipe out spotted owls regardless of what habitat protection accomplishes. And Tom Wheeler makes a point worth taking seriously: removals without restoring decades of lost habitat may not be enough on their own.

00:13:47.171 --> 00:13:57.811
<v Riley>That point matters more than the ethics argument. You can run the most rigorously permitted removal program in the country and still lose the species if the old-growth it depends on isn't there.

00:13:58.411 --> 00:14:11.587
<v Casey>But notice who's allowed near the problem. Not ranchers, not local hunters, not anyone with decades of watershed-level knowledge. Only someone holding a federal permit and species-identification training gets to act.

00:14:12.187 --> 00:14:28.363
<v Riley>Is that wrong, though? This isn't clearing a beaver dam off a culvert. Misidentify the bird in poor light and you've shot the animal the program exists to save. The credential exists because the skill is narrow and the error is fatal to the wrong owl.

00:14:28.907 --> 00:14:48.667
<v Casey>Sure. Structurally, though, it's the same shape as the tariff, with a different lock on the door. The mill can't act because market access sits in Washington. A local land manager can't act because the permit sits with a federal agency. Either way, whoever stands closest has no standing.

00:14:49.167 --> 00:14:56.087
<v Riley>One of those gatekeepers has a technical justification. The other is a trade dispute with no technical content whatsoever. Different complaints.

00:14:56.757 --> 00:15:11.941
<v Casey>Same structure, though. Competence and permission handed to whoever holds the license, everyone else told to wait. Sometimes that's justified. The tariff isn't — there's no skill threshold behind it, just a political one that moves with an election.

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:31.130
<v Riley>And forest tenure in this province runs on close to that same structure, and has for decades. A community forest agreement gives local operators cutting rights, but the province sets the allowable annual cut, approves the harvest plan, and holds final say on most operational decisions.

00:15:31.784 --> 00:15:48.464
<v Casey>When Quesnel and Williams Lake lost mills after the pine beetle salvage volume ran dry, that tenure structure is what left operators without a fallback. They didn't own the standing timber. They held a licence to cut it, on terms the province wrote and could change.

00:15:49.064 --> 00:16:00.256
<v Riley>Which is a real check on my own optimism. Better mill technology, better kilns, better grading — none of it changes who holds the tenure when conditions shift underneath them.

00:16:00.889 --> 00:16:16.009
<v Casey>Nation-held tenure is the interesting exception. When the entity managing the forest and the entity living with a bad harvest plan are the same entity, the incentives change, because there's no distant party to shift the cost onto.

00:16:16.579 --> 00:16:33.323
<v Riley>It also changes who's allowed to respond when something goes wrong on the ground. A Nation holding tenure outright doesn't wait on a ministry sign-off to adjust harvest practice after a fire or a beetle outbreak. That puts the permission question on ownership rather than on holding the right credential.

00:16:33.723 --> 00:16:45.131
<v Casey>Though Richmond Plywood doesn't get to pivot its whole product line overnight either, and that's not tenure. That's capital sunk in existing equipment and long-standing customer contracts.

00:16:45.796 --> 00:17:01.868
<v Riley>True, but a mill facing a trade shock has one lever a small tenure holder doesn't — it can shift product mix inside the licence it already holds. Panel producers leaning toward domestic construction, or mass timber for institutional buildings, don't need a new tenure. They need a new customer.

00:17:02.379 --> 00:17:11.323
<v Casey>On what timeline? Mass timber demand in Canada is real. It isn't absorbing a plywood mill's full volume in the six months before layoffs.

00:17:11.672 --> 00:17:17.144
<v Riley>(wry) No. The fix exists. It just arrives roughly a year after the funeral.

00:17:17.644 --> 00:17:31.452
<v Casey>Which is the honest version. Nobody in Quesnel gets to write a tariff exemption, and nobody outside a federal permit gets to touch the owl program. Neither clock runs at the speed a local economy needs.

00:17:32.063 --> 00:17:51.527
<v Riley>Agreed on the mismatch. The leverage question is where I'd lean forward. Nation-held tenure is a lever already moving rather than a hypothetical — as publicly reported, Indigenous governance over forest land in this region is expanding, and that shifts who holds final say on harvest decisions no matter what Ottawa or Washington do on trade.

00:17:52.050 --> 00:18:04.754
<v Casey>That's a governance shift, and it doesn't touch the tariff. A Nation holding tenure over standing timber still sells the finished panel into the same fifty-percent wall as everyone else.

00:18:05.354 --> 00:18:21.330
<v Riley>It doesn't solve the tariff. What it does is put the response — cut less this season, shift species mix, hold volume back and wait the dispute out — in the hands of people who live with the outcome, rather than an office in Victoria answering to a different set of pressures.

00:18:22.012 --> 00:18:31.476
<v Casey>That distinction holds. Owning the decision doesn't guarantee a good decision. It does mean whoever makes the call sleeps in the town where it lands.

00:18:32.007 --> 00:18:38.543
<v Riley>And the technology and the tenure model both matter without solving market access set five thousand kilometres away.

00:18:39.057 --> 00:18:56.705
<v Casey>On the owl side, the credential is doing what it should — nobody wants an untrained neighbour guessing at plumage in low light. What I'm left with is whether that same logic ever gets applied evenly, or whether the permission wall only ever tightens around the industries with the least power to argue back.

00:18:57.204 --> 00:19:36.076
<v Riley>Then the concrete ask locally is a seat at the table. The Cariboo Regional District, or a Nation-led forestry body, formally requesting standing when the province next reviews tenure terms tied to trade-exposed products — panel mills in particular, given what just landed on Richmond Plywood. That's a request a regional board could make this fall, and if you've got a stake in how tenure or trade exposure is actually playing out — mill worker, rancher watching cattle prices, someone inside a Nation-held forestry operation — send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We'd rather report what you're seeing than theorize about it.

00:19:36.591 --> 00:19:51.447
<v Casey>Two federal governments aren't going to settle a trade dispute because a podcast asked nicely. But knowing which decisions still get made in Victoria beats another round of hand-wringing about tariffs nobody here voted on. (wry) That's our Tuesday. Casey, out.

00:19:52.093 --> 00:19:56.541
<v Riley>Thanks for spending part of your day with us — and enjoy the clear sky while it lasts.